## What shipped

- **Three surfaces, one daemon.** A browser UI (all projects at once, or one), a
  terminal **TUI** across every project, and framework-agnostic web components you
  can embed in a web app — all over a single local, auth-less REST + SSE API.
- **Configurable terminal handoff.** With your terminal, shell, and coding agent
  set up, a ticket action starts that agent on the composed task in the selected
  repo. The daemon is local and has no user authentication.
- **Optional enrichment and Git history.** AI enrichment needs a configured
  provider or CLI. The default Git store commits edits on an orphan branch and
  supports revision-backed undo. Plain folder storage does not have Git history
  or that undo path.
- **Configurable evidence.** Before/after images and video can attach to a
  ticket when a media repository and publisher are configured. Installation
  alone does not grant hosted media publishing.

## Install

For the 0.1.3 browser UI, download the matching standalone binary from the
[pinned v0.1.3 release](https://github.com/aylith-labs/tickets/releases/tag/v0.1.3).
Linux and macOS have architecture-specific assets; Windows x64 has a separate
`.exe` asset. The `main/install.sh` convenience installer follows the latest
release and is not pinned to 0.1.3. The published 0.1.3 npm
archives include libraries and CLIs but omit the browser app assets, so npm is
not an equivalent browser installation for this release. The default Git-backed
`tickets init` path requires Git 2.42 or later; `tickets init --adapter folder`
avoids that requirement but has no Git revision history. After setup, run
`tickets serve` for the local browser UI or `tickets tui` for the terminal view.
